00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:37.179
But if only 1% of
savings by using IoT

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:41.320
is applied in oil and gas,
they could save $90 billion.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.090
Think about the impact this has.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:46.970
And overall IoT market
is a $3 trillion market.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:55.530
Now this is a perfect segue into
talking about Cloud IoT Core.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:00.090
This is a product which is
a fully-managed platform

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:06.180
for producing
insights from sensors

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.390
coming at a very rapid pace.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:14.250
Hundreds of thousands of
events happening in real time--

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.630
we are able to analyze that
using GCP and, specifically,

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.040
Cloud IoT Core.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.410
And using predictive
techniques as opposed

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.870
to time-based maintenance
can definitely

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:29.490
result in savings as well as
the safety of the operators.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.410
And there are certain
features here.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:36.280
I want to mention here
there are two components.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.920
There's a device
gateway, and there

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:45.180
are abilities to process
this data in real time.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.540
And there are some more features
that Google today announced,

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:51.330
so for that, I would like to
invite Prashant onstage to talk

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:52.860
about them.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.460
Prashant?

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:54.835
PRASHANT DHINGRA:
Thanks, Piyush.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:57.010
Hi.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:57.940
My name is Prashant Dhingra.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:59.830
I work as a machine
learning lead at Google.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:05.670
As Piyush mentioned,
Cloud IoT Core

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:06.940
is a fully-managed service.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.564
This is an entry point to
ingest data from Cloud IoT

00:10:09.564 --> 00:10:11.980
and then you can apply machine
learning or data processing

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.440
component.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.610
However, there is
a need to do data

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:18.460
processing where the data
exists, which is on the edge.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:21.730
So there is a research from
IDC that 40% of the data

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:25.390
needs to be processed
where it originated from.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:30.416
And there are six billion
devices get added every day.

00:10:30.416 --> 00:10:32.290
So there are a number
of reasons why you need

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.180
to process data at the edge.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.900
So latency and bandwidth
is the primary reason.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.990
For example, if you are doing
fraud detection on a credit

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.900
card, you want to
determine the fraud

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:45.440
as someone is swiping the card.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.100
So there is no time left to
take the data from credit card

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.710
and take it to the edge,
or take it to the Cloud

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.300
and then do the prediction.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.240
So there is a latency
requirement in many scenarios

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.390
where it is important to do
the prediction at the edge.

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:02.830
Similarly, if you have a data
in machinery or in aircraft

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.230
and ships, sometimes there
are latency requirements

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.600
that require immediate action.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.560
Bandwidth is also another issue.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:13.570
So if you are doing predictive
maintenance on a ship,

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:16.540
sometimes the ships are
in the sea and many times

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.330
during the wartime, they do
not have the right bandwidth

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:20.140
or connectivity.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.630
So there is a requirement
or a compliance requirement

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:26.740
that prediction should happen
even if there is no network

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:27.730
connectivity.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:30.370
There are privacy issues
also, like some of the data

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.910
is not safe to transmit
over the networks.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.140
That's another benefit of doing
the prediction on the edge.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:39.160
And as I mentioned,
connectivity might be an issue.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:42.259
Like when in a
machinery, the machines

00:11:42.259 --> 00:11:43.300
are not always connected.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.796
Ships or aircraft are
not always connected.

00:11:45.796 --> 00:11:47.170
And there are cost
benefits also.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.270
If you can do the
prediction on an edge,

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.970
you can save sending
all the data to a cloud.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.230
So these were the
driving factors

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:58.100
that Google took into
account and today they

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.830
announced ML on Edge.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.760
So in future, you will be
able to do data processing,

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:09.280
as well as you will be able
to do the machine learning on

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.600
as device.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.240
Even before today, there
were component availables,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:14.780
but they were not
managed component.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:16.690
For example, there's
a component called

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:20.260
TensorFlow Lite that takes
your machine learning model.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.270
You can compress the
machine learning model

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:24.220
and make it as smaller,
and you can deploy it

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.130
on an edge device.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:26.710
So even today, you can do it.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:29.260
But from tomorrow on, what
it will become much easier

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.630
for you, because there
will be a managed service.

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:34.870
And that managed service
is called Cloud IoT Edge.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:35.920
It has two pieces.

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.940
It has Edge ML
and Edge IoT Core.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:42.130
So earlier we discussed
Cloud IoT is a managed

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:44.950
service on a cloud, but
Edge ML and Edge IoT Core

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:47.320
will exist on your
devices, and you

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.120
will be able to
run it on Android

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.880
Things or any Linux-based
operating system.

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:57.420
So you might have also
heard about Edge TPUs.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.920
So I just wanted to call out
the difference between the two.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:04.360
Edge TPUs is a chip-based
hardware device,

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.940
and the Cloud IoT Edge
is a software stack,

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:08.530
so they work together.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.110
So in a Cloud IoT
Edge, you can decide

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.210
to do data processing
or machine learning

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.700
using CPU, GPUs, or TPUs.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:19.720
So if you have a requirement
where you need to use TPUs,

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.360
you can use Edge
TPUs to do prediction

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:24.830
on very complex model.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.310
So your overall architecture
will look like that.

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:30.250
You will do the
Edge ML on a device,

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.550
and then you will take that
data, take it to Cloud.

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.530
You will do the training
there on the aggregated data,

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:41.010
you will build a model, and
deploy it on various devices.

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:44.990
This is a more complex version
of the previous ML lifecycle.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:46.340
So which component you will use?

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:49.190
You will use Edge ML and
Edge IoT Core on a device.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:53.120
Cloud IoT Core is for
connectivity of device,

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:55.460
and you can use IoT
function or Pub/Sub

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:58.460
to aggregate the data or
Dataflow to process the data.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:02.120
And then you can store the
data into a Bigtable, BigQuery,

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.310
and once you have
stored the data,

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:07.010
you can do training using
Cloud Machine Learning.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.320
So this will be an
end-to-end architecture.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:11.540
And using this, you
will be able to build

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.240
powerful predictive maintenance
model, which you can run it

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.196
on a cloud as well as you can
deploy it on an Edge device.

00:14:18.196 --> 00:14:19.405
That's all, Piyush.

00:14:19.405 --> 00:14:20.405
PIYUSH MALIK: All right.

00:14:20.405 --> 00:14:20.560
PRASHANT DHINGRA: Thanks a lot.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:21.559
PIYUSH MALIK: Thank you.

00:14:21.559 --> 00:14:23.090
Thank you, Prashant.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:24.060
This is awesome.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:27.110
I mean, we got the latest
information coming out

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.379
of Google from this
morning, and we

00:14:29.379 --> 00:14:31.420
were able to incorporate
during the presentation.

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:32.480
So that's awesome.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.150
Now lets talk about a solution
that we at springML have

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:38.540
built using IoT Core and ML.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:42.402
And what are the problems that--

00:14:42.402 --> 00:14:44.360
again, going back to the
predictive maintenance

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.040
scenario.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.500
If somebody has to
maintain equipment,

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:51.000
they've got to keep spare parts.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:51.840
Right?

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.750
If you have too much of
spare parts available,

00:14:54.750 --> 00:15:00.010
that's like capital
bound, or wasted capital.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:01.570
You want to optimize on that.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.840
And if you don't have the
right component available

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:06.360
when a machine
breakdown happens,

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:09.480
then again, there is a
loss of productivity.

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:11.370
So how do you get around this?

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:14.610
And this is a solution
we will show how,

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:16.920
and we'll give you
a demo as well, how

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.550
it brings it together.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:21.150
So as we have been
talking about,

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:25.500
sensors in the components
in the machines talk.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.650
All we have to do is
listen to the machines.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:35.050
The machines are telling
you when they need help.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.310
Not time-based.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:40.390
It's not like your car has
an auto meter of 10,000 miles

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:41.410
and it needs oil change.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:42.940
No, not anymore.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:46.420
Or for that matter, any boat
that is traveling in sea,

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:49.990
and it needs change
of an engine,

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:52.750
because it has run
for so many hours.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:56.390
It is the components
which look at wear

00:15:56.390 --> 00:16:02.090
and tear, look at temperature,
humidity, and so on, so forth.

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:04.120
And this solution
brings all of that

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:09.010
together using the concept
of remaining useful life.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.220
And again, we used
Cloud IoT Core,

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:17.020
and the sensor data was read in
a secure fashion in real time,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:20.810
and we executed machine learning
model to get predictions.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:24.160
And this is a
simplified architecture.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.050
It might look a bit--

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:29.090
let me just simplify it for you.

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:31.120
The gateway-- the
sensor data comes in.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:36.300
Cloud Pub/Sub, which can handle
lots of events per second

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.500
in hundreds of
thousands, and it is

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:41.410
able to process the data
through the pipeline

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:44.740
and various components for
either storing or doing

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:48.230
analytics on the Cloud,
and it can be deployed

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.450
for the users [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:54.430
So with that, of course,
this benefit of this solution

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:57.460
is it provides deep
understanding of your asset

00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:02.330
performance in real time, and
proactive alerts are sent out.

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:05.770
So with that, I want to
invite my colleague Manuel

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:09.279
to give a demo of the solution
that we built. There you go.

00:17:09.279 --> 00:17:10.656
MANUEL AMUNTAGUEI: Thank you.

00:17:10.656 --> 00:17:11.612
Thank you.

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:18.849
Can I get the
slides back please?

00:17:22.076 --> 00:17:23.920
Thank you.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:28.150
So I'm going to walk you through
a demo we built around the oil

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:33.610
refinery industry, and
it's to showcase IoT Core

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:37.187
and also how it fits in the GCP.

00:17:37.187 --> 00:17:39.520
It turns out, one thing that
was interesting in building

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.890
this demo was to
understand the big picture.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.740
It turns out IoT Core is
actually not the bottleneck.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:45.690
It works really well.

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:46.660
It's very easy to use.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.710
You plug-in your sensors
and you get your data.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:50.960
The problem is you end
up with a deluge of data.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:51.960
There's just too much--

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:52.910
an avalanche of data.

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:54.410
And how do you deal with it?

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:57.980
And that's when the whole GCP
pipeline starts making sense.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:00.710
Especially if you want to
run IoT models at scale,

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:04.570
you need all the
pieces that fit in

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:07.120
after IoT Core have
to be able to scale,

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:08.770
have to be able to
reduce your data,

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:11.186
have to be able to deal with
it so you can learn about it.

00:18:11.186 --> 00:18:14.180
So first off,
before I go to demo,

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:16.220
let me just talk about
some of the challenges.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:20.360
You need enough historical
data to run these models.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.220
In our case, we're
looking at time series.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:23.820
It's like survival analysis.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.550
So you'll need enough
data from the time

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:28.159
you turn the machine on to
the time the machine dies.

00:18:28.159 --> 00:18:30.200
You need to have enough
of these historical cases

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:32.570
in order to run these
kind of models--

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:36.370
to train these kind of models
to learn what features indicates

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:37.460
a failure.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.335
And if it's not time
series-- if it's binary,

00:18:39.335 --> 00:18:40.793
you'll need enough
of your whatever

00:18:40.793 --> 00:18:42.391
the minority cases
in order to learn.

00:18:42.391 --> 00:18:43.890
If it's
multi-classification, you'll

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.520
need to have enough
of each class

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.712
to be able to properly
train a model.

00:18:48.712 --> 00:18:50.420
And that's nothing to
do with IoT, right?

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.090
That's just machine
learning in general.

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:56.199
Same thing with selecting
the right model.

00:18:56.199 --> 00:18:57.740
Because we're talking
about big data,

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.140
we're talking about
big data streaming,

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:02.600
you've got to find models
that work in the Cloud

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:06.529
that you can mount easily
in Cloud MLE that can handle

00:19:06.529 --> 00:19:07.820
this huge amount of throughput.

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:10.550
And I'll talk about
this after the demo.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:14.010
And of course, what
is your end product?

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.800
Is it going to be a dashboard?

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:16.790
An alerting system?

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:19.650
Does it feed into
some other tool?

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.350
So all these things have to be
thought of as a big picture.

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:25.160
The IoT is more than
just the IoT Core.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:27.730
Its a lot of different pieces.

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.410
And can I have my
machine now please?

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:36.260
So here I'm in Google
Cloud and I have

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.780
Virtual Machine ready to go.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:42.244
And I'm going to simulate
some machine data.

00:19:42.244 --> 00:19:44.660
And I'll talk about where that
data came from in a minute.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:45.493
We're going to wait.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:51.200
It's going to
generate two engines,

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:57.510
and it's going to assimilate a
bunch of sensors per machine.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.760
I will give it a minute.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:00.260
I mean, a couple of
seconds till it starts.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:00.760
There it is.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:03.060
There it comes in.

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:04.960
And now I can go to the demo.

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.710
And we have a website.

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:07.220
There it is.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:07.720
So yeah.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:12.839
So the two points-- so you could
have 1,000 of these oil rigs.

00:20:12.839 --> 00:20:14.630
So each green dot on
the map is an oil rig.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:15.963
We have two just for this demo--

00:20:15.963 --> 00:20:17.269
North Dakota and Kansas.

00:20:17.269 --> 00:20:18.560
And I can click on any of them.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:20.790
I'll choose the one in Kansas.

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:22.820
And then you see a
green dot that appears.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:23.320
Here.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.419
I'll try to use a laser.

00:20:24.419 --> 00:20:25.460
A green dot that appears.

00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:26.910
You could have multiple
machines on this rig.

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:28.130
Again, for the
simplicity of this demo,

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:29.270
we're just using one.

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:32.020
And here we click on it.

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:36.060
And in this case, just for
the closure of this demo,

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:38.100
we're building a dashboard.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.680
So the top part, you
have the predictive side.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.710
So this is what the engine
learned, and now it's

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:47.160
saying that this
engine currently

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:48.360
has plenty of life left.

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.180
So normally these engines
would break down in years.

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.490
Of course here we're doing
it in minutes for the demo.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.729
But as long as it's in
the green zone, it's safe.

00:20:55.729 --> 00:20:57.270
If it gets to the
yellow, that's when

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:58.860
you would fire off alerts--

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:01.200
text or whatever
needs to be done,

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.080
or a temporary turn down
a machine to swap a piece.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:06.150
When it's in danger
zone, it's just bad.

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:09.770
So the second level here, these
are the real-time indicators.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:13.810
It tells you basically
what the machine--

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:16.190
the ML model is using.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:17.940
This is the same data,
so we're presenting

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:20.846
real-time data like cable
tension, oil temperature,

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:21.720
et cetera, et cetera.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.450
That's what the model
is using to predict

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:27.270
the above bar, whether it's
healthy or not healthy,

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:28.200
or in danger.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:31.060
And lower we have a bunch
of historical indicators,

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:34.110
and that's just recording
the data through time

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:39.360
so that we can see how it's
progressing through time

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:41.610
and before failure.

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:45.970
And I am going to
go back to the map.

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:48.940
And I'll bring it back when
it's in a yellow state.

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.440
But can I get the
slides back please?

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.430
Thank you.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:56.800
So high-level-- there's
a deeper view later on.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:00.780
But a very high level, you
have the Python simulator that

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.480
is pumping data into IoT Core--

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:06.410
so these are the sensors--

00:22:06.410 --> 00:22:07.080
into IoT Core.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:11.070
IoT Core then messages
it out to Pub/Sub.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:13.860
Pub/Sub is this massive
message queuing system,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:18.409
and publishes it out, and we
have Dataflow as a subscriber.

00:22:18.409 --> 00:22:19.950
So when you have a
subscriber, you're

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:22.260
always guaranteed
to get every packet

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:23.640
at least once from Pub/Sub.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.800
That's the guarantee
it does-- the contract.

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:27.690
And Dataflow can do a lot.

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:29.280
Here we're just
scratching the surface

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:30.450
of what Dataflow can do.

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:33.302
It can do aggregation, it
can do data transformation,

00:22:33.302 --> 00:22:35.010
it can do MapReduce--
that kind of stuff.

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:35.970
So these are tools.

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:40.050
All these tools are
designed to scale and handle

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:41.140
huge amounts of data.

00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:43.050
So it's not just
IoT Core anymore.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:44.760
It's everything in
the pipeline that

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.040
needs to be able to handle
what you're throwing at it.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.900
And Dataflow will
transform the data,

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.940
pass it to the Regressor model--
here the deep neural network

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:53.160
regressor--

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:54.960
to get a prediction,
and all that data

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:56.010
is stored in BigQuery.

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:59.640
And BigQuery-- massive
data warehouse.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:04.680
I was reading you could
query 35 billion rows of data

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.360
with no indexes in seconds.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:10.050
And it just-- it's funny because
we work a lot with BigQuery,

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:13.770
and you're typing select star
or select whatever field from,

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:16.740
and it just feels like you're
querying your local database

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:18.700
on your machine, and
you're like, eh, whatever.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:19.200
Right?

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:19.620
It ran.

00:23:19.620 --> 00:23:20.170
It's good.

00:23:20.170 --> 00:23:21.836
When in reality,
what's happening behind

00:23:21.836 --> 00:23:23.760
is it could be querying
thousands of machines

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:24.930
all around the
world, and it just

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:26.388
feels like it's
your local machine.

00:23:26.388 --> 00:23:28.170
So that's a phenomenal tool.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:31.104
Great to work with.

00:23:31.104 --> 00:23:33.270
And I need to talk a bit
about the data we're using.

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:37.260
So we're using this dataset
called Modular Aero-Propulsion

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:38.160
System Simulations--

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.794
MAPSS.

00:23:39.794 --> 00:23:41.460
It's open source made
available by NASA.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:45.837
And these are turbofan
engines from life to death,

00:23:45.837 --> 00:23:46.920
and they made that public.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:51.210
So we are simulating
our data based on that,

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:53.280
and that was very useful
for us, because we're

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:58.390
talking about real machine
data with a series of sensors.

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:01.720
And there are 22 in total.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:05.770
And here you would train
your model for IoT,

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:07.570
just like you would
train any other model.

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:09.695
You'll figure out if your
features are significant.

00:24:09.695 --> 00:24:11.625
You'll figure out
variable importance.

00:24:11.625 --> 00:24:13.000
And for example,
in this case, we

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:14.916
found a bunch that were
not helping the model.

00:24:14.916 --> 00:24:16.750
We threw them out,
and we just kept

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:20.530
the ones that were of use,
just like you would model

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:22.900
anything-- any other model.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:28.460
In terms of the models we used--
so we tried a whole bunch.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:29.530
This is time series data.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:32.485
So we tried LSTMs, we tried
Wide and Deep Regressor.

00:24:32.485 --> 00:24:35.650
It's a very popular model that
is part of the Estimator API.

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:40.450
But in the end, the estimator
DNNRegressor was the simplest.

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:43.000
Really transparent model,
and it worked really well.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:45.490
And I'll say a few words
here about the Estimator API,

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.410
because this is really
huge in TensorFlow.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:50.920
Most of the TensorFlow
code you'll see online

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:55.240
may not fit easily into the
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.170
And when you're talking
about modeling at scale,

00:24:58.170 --> 00:24:59.790
or predicting at
scale, you've got

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:03.130
to make sure that your model
can be distributed to thousands

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:04.720
of machines if needs be.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.660
So you need to comply
with the way Cloud MLE can

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:09.220
handle your TensorFlow model.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:13.720
And if you follow the
Estimator-- the API--

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:15.610
you'll automatically
get inherited classes.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:17.872
The deployment classes,
the train and test classes,

00:25:17.872 --> 00:25:18.580
all these things.

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:20.390
And even if you want
to do a more complex,

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:23.770
it does have a bunch
of built-in models

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.180
like DNNRegressor
and a Wide and Deep.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:29.150
But if you need to do really
complex custom models,

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:32.590
you can still inherit from the
base class of TF Estimator,

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:34.690
and you will benefit
from the exact functions

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.890
you need to be able to
deploy to the Cloud.

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:41.470
So can I have my machine
back for a minute please?

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:42.880
So just want to highlight that.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:46.750
So now both of these
machines are in alert state.

00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:49.160
So this would be when you
would start sending out

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:54.160
SMSs, texts, update dashboards--
you know, fire off alarms.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:55.865
Can I have the
slides back please?

00:25:55.865 --> 00:25:57.240
And eventually
it'll turn to red,

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:01.710
but I think I don't need
to [INAUDIBLE] on that.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:03.460
And I'll talk about a
scoring in a minute,

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:04.550
so I'm going leave that one.

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.716
So here is how you would
call this Regressor model--

00:26:06.716 --> 00:26:09.490
part of the Estimator API.

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:11.200
It's super simple.

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:13.990
You just call it, and
all you need really

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:16.240
is just give it
your hidden units.

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:17.670
And I think even
will do a default

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.030
to hidden units for you.

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:21.040
But you can say how
many hidden layers

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.690
you want-- the sizes you want.

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:24.250
Here we are calling
in the optimizer.

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:25.840
You don't even have to do
that if you don't want to,

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:27.423
but it's nice, because
you can pass it

00:26:27.423 --> 00:26:29.452
your L1 and your learning rate.

00:26:29.452 --> 00:26:31.035
But you wouldn't
even need to do that.

00:26:31.035 --> 00:26:35.441
So that permits you to not worry
too much about mucking around

00:26:35.441 --> 00:26:37.690
with this stuff and think
about what your business use

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:39.398
case is-- what's really
important, right?

00:26:39.398 --> 00:26:41.980
Because these things
work, they're well done,

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:45.165
you can trust them, and they do
expose a series of parameters

00:26:45.165 --> 00:26:46.540
that you need,
but you don't have

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:47.990
to worry too much
above the rest.

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:50.420
And this really
frees us to say, OK,

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.570
we know it's from
the Estimator API.

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:54.920
It's going to be able to
be mounted in Cloud MLE.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:55.420
Fine.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:58.330
And you can move on and think
about what your business is all

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.070
about.

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:03.440
In terms of score, I'm going to
just briefly talk about this.

00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:07.690
We've got a root mean
squared error of 45.

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:12.490
That means there's about 350
on average cycles per engine

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.450
in our demo data.

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:19.090
And that means we're plus or
minus correct on 45 cycles.

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:21.199
And Prashant was just
telling me that they

00:27:21.199 --> 00:27:22.990
worked on this model
and this dataset more,

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:24.780
and they even got
it down to five.

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:27.899
So more future engineering.

00:27:27.899 --> 00:27:29.440
You can work this
number a lot lower.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:32.390
But this was in line with the
papers on that dataset we saw,

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:36.060
so we were satisfied
just for the demo's sake.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:41.520
OK, so here is a more
precise schema of the model.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:46.020
So you see the Python is passing
the simulated data to the IoT

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:46.520
Core.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:47.936
IoT Core is passing
it to Pub/Sub.

00:27:47.936 --> 00:27:49.530
Pub/Sub is passing
it to Dataflow.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.988
So this is where we see more
precision than the other chart

00:27:51.988 --> 00:27:52.997
are showing you before.

00:27:52.997 --> 00:27:54.330
Dataflow can do a lot of things.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:58.680
It can be like your data
manager, your choreographer

00:27:58.680 --> 00:27:59.610
here in this case.

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.920
So it's doing multiple things.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.290
It's preparing the data
so that you can pass it

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:07.350
to the DNNRegressor model,
which is mounted in Google Cloud

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:10.160
through Cloud MLE.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.370
So this is the prediction.

00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:14.170
And it's also passing the
real-time data to BigQuery.

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:17.410
BigQuery, as I mentioned, is
an industrial-strength data

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:17.910
warehouse.

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:18.900
It's a bottomless pit.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:20.483
You can just keep
throwing data at it.

00:28:20.483 --> 00:28:22.330
It will just distribute
in the background.

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:24.960
So that's when you start
seeing these more sophisticated

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:25.680
models.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.420
It's no more you have one
model yielding a prediction.

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:31.800
Now you have many
models, and it could

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:34.350
have many models-- many
different processes handling

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:34.980
the data.

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:37.500
And the output is everything.

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:40.390
It's going to be BigQuery is
going to give our visualization

00:28:40.390 --> 00:28:40.890
layer.

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:42.348
That dashboard I
was showing you is

00:28:42.348 --> 00:28:44.680
going to give the prediction.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:45.780
That was the top part.

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:49.470
It's also going to give
the real-time data.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.870
So because we're pumping
all the data into BigQuery,

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.900
you just take whatever features
you have, the latest timestamp,

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:58.380
and those are your real-time
values for your features

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:00.120
of whatever machine
you're looking at.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:03.060
And also historical data, right?

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:04.796
And all that can be
queried in BigQuery.

00:29:04.796 --> 00:29:07.170
So BigQuery is actually yielding
three different values--

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:09.180
the predictions,
the current values,

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:10.590
and the historical data.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.480
So pretty powerful tool.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:13.770
Very easy to work with.

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:15.750
And in terms of
training, I'll close this

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:18.480
by saying a few things.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:21.060
If an engine is going
to break down on average

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:23.340
every three years,
maybe every six months

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:24.690
you could retrain, right?

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.330
If the model you
feel is drifting,

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:29.670
you could also build some
continuous online modeling

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:33.140
where it's continually
reassessing the data.

00:29:33.140 --> 00:29:34.890
The only caveat, and
something that we all

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:37.098
have to think about when
we're building these models,

00:29:37.098 --> 00:29:39.514
is if you retrain
on a model where

00:29:39.514 --> 00:29:40.680
you have done something on--

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:42.550
you have taken action
on a prediction--

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:43.800
your data becomes tainted.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:45.500
Not tainted, but
it's just different.

00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:48.000
So you need to
segregate what data

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:49.590
you have taken an
action on because

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.686
of a prediction from your model,
versus what was left alone.

00:29:52.686 --> 00:29:54.060
So if you're doing
another round,

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:56.280
you probably would have
to do two separate models,

00:29:56.280 --> 00:30:00.070
or just choose which one
you want to work with.

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:03.110
And this is just a quick
recap over what we saw.

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:04.900
So this is in a healthy,
healthy machine.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:07.360
Everything looks like
it's in the green.

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.520
We have the historical values.

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:11.530
And now here we
have it is broken.

00:30:11.530 --> 00:30:13.140
This is in the
danger zone, and we

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:14.950
can see we have some
extremes, and that's

00:30:14.950 --> 00:30:16.849
what the model would use.

00:30:16.849 --> 00:30:18.390
We started with 17
features, so there

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:21.180
are more than just those four.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:22.120
And that's it.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:24.290
I'm going to pass
it back to Piyush.

